  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... Patchy morning fog, high humidity, hot \ntemperatures, and daily thunderstorm chances continue through \nearly next week. \n\nSomewhat similar conditions to what occurred today are expected\nSaturday as another potent shortwave passes through. The main \ndifference is that mid-level flow turns a bit more W to WSW \nversus the SW'ly flow we have had in recent days. As a result, \ncoverage of storms is likely to be less than what we saw today.\nHowever, shear values increase a bit, reaching 30 to 35 kts as \nthe subtropical jet stream begins to strengthen. Mid- level \nlapse rates are also impressive, similar to today's event. \nInstability will be a bit less tomorrow, owing to the westerly \nmid-level flow. Thermodynamic profiles depict skinnier CAPE \nprofiles, but inverted-V signatures towards the surface. Our \nthoughts are that the coverage should be less tomorrow, but the \nceiling could be higher in terms of damaging wind potential, \nshould storms be able to develop and get organized. The Storm \nPrediction Center has added a Marginal Risk for severe weather \ntomorrow as a result that encompasses much of the forecast area,\nfurther reinforcing our thoughts. \n\nConvection begins to wane a bit Sunday into Monday given quasi-zonal \nflow aloft. A few storms may develop over the higher elevations,\nbut coverage will be much less than in recent days. This will \nbe short-lived however, with storm chances increasing once \nagain Tuesday through the end of the week as a series of \nshortwave disturbances pass through. \n\nOutside of the storm chances expect highs in the upper 80s and low \n90s with heat indices east of the Blue Ridge up around 100 \ndegrees. The hottest days look to be Sunday through Tuesday \ngiven the strengthened subtropical ridge over the region.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...A cold front could arrive late next week and \ninto the weekend bringing relief from the heat and humidity. \n\nWhile this is very far out in the forecast period, there is some \nsignal that a more potent frontal system could push through the \nregion late in the week. The details are murky at this stage of the \nforecast, with timing very uncertain, as well as potential impacts. \nDeterministic and ensemble guidance are generally between \nThursday/Friday right now, with improvement in the weather \npattern possible into the upcoming weekend. Any decent frontal \npassage in the summertime should be watched closely for \npotential severe weather, so we will keep monitoring this in the\ncoming days to see if that potential is there, and try to \nnarrow down the timeframe a bit more.",
